 @jamescelliers3195  At £20 of difference, I can definitely understand the sentiment. However, if you caved and bought it at the height of the shortage, then you'd probably be looking at closer to £200 minimum lost at the very least unless you got lucky. It's even worse at the lower end where cards that used to cost $100 still go for double on eBay and that $100 could have been better spent elsewhere.

Personally, I rarely play AAA releases new if at all, so I'm content to sit it out until the price inflation is around 30-40% if it doesn't budge from that. I could bite the bullet and shrug my shoulders going, "Such is life." then, but in my current situation where I'm trying to rebuild everything from scratch, throwing away an extra $400 now for a still used card when I could hold my breath and delay gratification for at least $200 of savings goes a long way.

Also, just a general reply and in no way directed to you, I sometimes think that PC gamers have a skewed idea of what's acceptable performance and can't tell the difference between what's just old and what's actually unusable. The 900 series is starting to skirt the latter category even with the 980Ti while the 20 series (and most of the 10 series) all-around is perfectly usable for a lot of titles even at high settings. As someone whose been on both sides of the hardware spectrum, it's a sad sentiment to see.

 @EbonySaints  I literally brought the card when it was the most expensive just after launch. I got the Aorus rx6700xt that has lots of rgb and a heft cooler which cost more than some of the other 6700xt's. And like I said its devalued by £20 after a year. I'm in the UK and using pounds, not dollars or euros. Yeah if I got one one of the budget rx6700xts then it would have devalued more and would cost loser to £600 new now, but I've got the Aorus card (at the time the budget 6700xts were going for about £750, so I only spent £50 extra for a better card).

I had a rx 580 8gb that I sold for £300 (it cost me £200 like 4 years earlier lol), so that made the card for more reasonable, it actually cost 500 + my old 580, which is only a little bit more than it would have cost if there was no inflated prices or low supply (in which case I would only have been able to sell the 580 for £100 instead of £300, and the rx6700xt would have cost about £500, so in the end it only cost me like £600 when if prices were good it would have cost me £500.


I was planning on upgrading before prices went crazy and already upgraded my monitor to a 1440p 144hz while i was waiting for rdna2 and 30 series card to launch so needed the new card to power that resolution and refresh rate. I was having to play games at 1080p on my 1440p monitor with the 580 and it was wasted potential. I only played apex legends at the time but now have a vr headset which definitely needs the extra power (it runs games at 5k resolution). It all depends on what you need it to do, for 60fps at 1080p then a rx580/1060 would have been fine for a bit longer. Its nice not having to worry about gpu power at all and knowing i can crank graphics and framerate to the max.

